      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. so if the LDL is building up in my arteries, how does that actually happen? like what is it doing to the walls of the blood vessels? i'm just trying to picture it so i understand the risk.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of the process like this: when you consume food, especially fatty foods, your body breaks it down into a substance called triglycerides. LDL then picks up some of these triglycerides and carries them to where they're needed, which is usually to cells. But if it can't deliver all the triglycerides to cells, it'll deposit the excess into the walls of your arteries, causing inflammation. This inflammation can lead to the build-up of plaque, which eventually hardens and narrows the arteries, makin' it harder for blood to flow through.",
      "source": "target",
